Z||000002||Old Lar||11-05-2001||08:55 PM||||Although the SHO from Ford is an impressive engine it will be lots of work to get it to work in a Fiero.  Some have tried, I have not heard of any successes.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/SHO2M_2.jpg"> <P>Check out your wallet to see the cash for any engine swap.  Not going to be cheap.  If you have lots of time to do it and money you can put just about any engine into a Fiero.  If you haven't done it before, get lots of instructions from the experts.  V8 s require a kit for the install.  $2-3K from Archie.  Noting is just bolt and go.  1984 will require some wiring modifications.||||reg||1
Z||000003||blazin'||11-05-2001||09:16 PM||||I'm thinking about the adapter kit from Archie. I've yet to hear a bad remark about his kits.<P>I don't plan on doing this project as fast as possible, I know it's gonna be expensive, but still something to look forward to! ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Borgio||11-05-2001||10:43 PM||||If you want more power, you can put a 3.1 or 3.4 in it, both are 60* engines, like the 2.8.  There are different varieties of these. I plan to put a 3.4 in mine. It bolts right in, except you have to drill holes and mount the starter on the other side. If you want more power, you can put a supercharged 3800. This isn't as good of a fit, it is a 90* V6. And if still, you want more power a V8 is the way to go, possibly a 350, even harder to fit in. Some people put a northstar in theirs. I think there lighter and higher strung. Hope this helps. Hope I'm right and have some idea what I'm talking about. This is just what I've heard.||||reg||1
